AND THE EARLIER NUMBERS FOR THIS HUNK WERE INFLATED, WHICH IS THE POINT OF
RE-MEASURING. Before br-r37-c1-mgrevstore fixed the kernel, this same routing
measured 0.9685x and 0.9037x. On a CORRECT kernel it measures 0.6064x and
0.6405x. The difference is exactly the work the broken kernel was skipping: it
defaulted every weight to 1 on a store-backed graph instead of reading the
store, so it looked fast because it was not doing the job. Those pre-fix figures
are void and must not be cited.

Note the contrast with the scalar hunk in the same commit, whose pre-fix numbers
re-measured ACCURATE (0.1034 -> 0.9861 then, 0.1037 -> 0.9889 now) because its
fixture used int weights on a PLAIN graph, a shape the broken kernel handled
correctly. Same suspicion, two different answers - which is why each had to be
re-run rather than reasoned about.

CONTROLS. `out_degree(n)` unweighted is flat on both classes (1.1828/1.2234 ->
1.1734/1.1633 and 0.5872/0.5701 -> 0.5889/0.5735). DiGraph weighted in/out is
flat (0.9992/0.9936 -> 0.9945/0.9861 and 0.9673/0.9629 -> 0.9529/0.9522), which
is what the `type(self._graph) is MultiDiGraph` gate is for: routing DiGraph
here measured SLOWER in the original probe, so it is deliberately excluded.

`degree(n,w) total` is listed as a CONTROL by this bench and moves
